# Tests

The test layers are in `docs/design.ja.md` §12.1. This rule is how a test is written.

## Framework

- xUnit v3 with its built-in `Assert`. No assertion library.
- Tests run on Microsoft.Testing.Platform (`global.json` `test.runner`). The .NET 10 SDK refuses VSTest for these projects, so there is no `Microsoft.NET.Test.Sdk` or `xunit.runner.visualstudio`. Run one project with `dotnet test --project <path>`, and some of its tests with `dotnet test --project <path> -- --filter-method <pattern>`. The test executable takes xUnit's own options (`-method`); given `--filter-method`, it prints `error: unknown option`, runs no test, and exits 3. In L1b a loop that looked only for failed tests counted 30 such runs as passes. Read the exit code and the count of tests run.
- Before calling a failing test a defect, look for what a mutation run leaves (`git status`, `*.orig` beside the source) and build again. In L1b a `wrap-reverse` test failed on a build that still had a mutant in it, and the defect it pointed to did not exist.
- A test checked by a mutant reads the mutant's build first: a mutant that does not compile leaves the last binary, and its tests pass. In V1c the first mutant of the hover's reentrancy left a field unused, the build failed on that warning, and the test passed (PR #327).
- A mutation script gives each test run a time limit and counts a run past it as a kill; it reports a mutant whose pattern matched other than once, or whose build failed, as not run, and the mutant is fixed and run before it counts. In V3a the mutant whose `DetachChildren` left each child's parent set hung the whole Core suite for 30 minutes before the run was stopped by hand, and three of the ten tree mutants first did not run: two patterns matched twice, and one build failed on CS0162, code that cannot be reached (PR #369). T13's mutants that wrote `if (false)` and `if (true)` failed the same way; a condition that is false but not constant (`node.IsNone`, `x.Length < 0`) builds (PR #438).
- A mutation script reads and writes the sources by absolute paths, and sets the time of a source it puts back to now (`.cursor/rules/powershell-shell.mdc`). T12 broke both: after a `cd`, `[IO.File]::ReadAllText` read a path in another worktree and the first three mutants did not run; then the sources put back with `Copy-Item` kept their old time, and the gate's Debug build tested a mutant's assembly, where six tests failed (PR #426).
- A test of an invariant (`InvariantViolationException`) runs in the Debug configuration only, because release builds end the process. Start it with a `RequireDebugBuild()` helper that calls `Assert.Skip` when `DEBUG` is not defined (`tests/XylemUI.Core.Tests/ArenaTests.cs`). The gate runs both configurations. In T5 two new invariant tests had no skip, and the Release run of the gate ended the D3D11 test process at the first one (PR #228).
- Process-wide state (`UiThread`, the native handle table, Win32 windows) is touched only from one dedicated thread per test assembly: the UI thread of `XylemUI.Testing`'s `HeadlessSession`, which a `[UiFact]` test runs on and `UiThreadFixture` (`tests/Shared`, linked into every test project by `tests/Directory.Build.props`) hands a test's code to. xUnit v3 runs each test assembly in its own process. A second UI thread throws in `UiThread.Initialize`, so a test project keeps no fixture of its own: six copies of the fixture each made one before P02a (Issue #460).
- Time and frames come from a fake `TimeProvider` and the test frame scheduler (design §12.2). A test never sleeps, never waits on the wall clock, and never asserts what the clock decides.
- The exception is a test of how the OS itself waits and wakes (the Win32 message loop, S3). It is marked `[Trait("Category", "OsTiming")]`, waits in real time with bounds an order of magnitude above what was measured, and has a watchdog that wakes and fails a loop that does not finish, so that it cannot hang the run (`RunLoop` and `LoopWatchdog` in `tests/XylemUI.Platform.Windows.Tests/LoopTestHelpers.cs`).
- A test that wakes the loop from another thread owns that thread and joins it before the test ends (`WakeTicker` in `LoopTestHelpers.cs`). A wake queued to the thread pool outlives the test and reaches the next one: in S3 a leftover `ThreadPool` wake woke the idle test on CI, which ran the tests in another order than the local run, and the measured delay came out negative. A test that waits for its own wake also counts and asserts the iterations before it, so a stray wake fails loudly instead of being measured.
- The loop checks a test's done condition only when it wakes. When another thread sets that condition, it wakes the loop after setting it. Without the wake the loop stays blocked and the watchdog fails the test: the `Send` tests of S5 did this until the worker called `Win32MessageLoop.Wake` at its end.
- `Win32Window(title, width, height)` takes the outer size; the client area is smaller (200 x 100 gives 184 x 61). A test that reads pixels or places content by coordinates asserts `ClientSize` first (S7).
- `TestContext.Current` is async-local and does not flow to the fixture's UI thread. Read `TestContext.Current.TestOutputHelper` on the test thread before `ui.Invoke`. To see the output of a passing test, run the test executable with `-showLiveOutput`.
- A test that needs an OS font or component skips, with its name, when the machine lacks it, and calls that check for each one it assumes (`SkipWithout` in `tests/XylemUI.Platform.Windows.Tests/DirectWriteFontProviderTests.cs`). In F1 two tests assumed Segoe UI Emoji without the check and would fail on Server Core (PR #152 review).
- Tests that need a GPU (layer 4) use WARP and run on Windows only; they are marked so the other layers run on every OS.
- A test in a real window that runs hundreds of frames (a Zero-Alloc check) hands the window a target that draws and presents nothing, unless what it checks needs the GPU: V3c's allocation test presented about 1,120 frames on WARP, 19.5 s on the machine, and on the CI's Windows runner `XylemUI.Desktop.Tests` stopped for 27 minutes until the job's 30-minute limit ended it (PR #381).
- When the Windows job fails in `XylemUI.Desktop.Tests`, download the artifact `hangdump-<attempt>` (`gh run download <run> -n hangdump-<attempt>`) before running the job again, and write the tests that its `_hang.log` names on #415 (row Q5). A run again leaves the hang it had unread (PR #428). The dump is full (`--hangdump-type Full`): a dump of the heap alone holds no metadata of the modules, and `dotnet-dump` named the stuck thread's methods `Unknown` (PR #432's run). Read it without installing the tool: `dotnet dnx -y dotnet-dump -- analyze <dump> -c "setsymbolserver -clear" -c "setsymbolserver -cache <cache>" -c "clrstack -all"`. Leave Microsoft's symbol server, which the tool sets by default, only for the first read of a runtime version, which fetches the runtime's DAC into the cache; with the server set, the tool may fetch the symbols of every module (`.agents/skills/cpu-profile/SKILL.md`, PR #434).
- A Core type that the tests on Linux and macOS make calls no native library (HarfBuzz, until rows P04 and P06 build it there) in its constructor or field initializers; a native call goes where the text that needs it is made. The local gate runs on Windows only, so such a call first fails in CI: V1d's `UiTree` made its UI language with `TextLanguage.FromTag` in an initializer, and every Core test that made a tree threw `DllNotFoundException` on Linux and macOS (PR #340's first CI). `TextShaper` made its HarfBuzz buffer in a field initializer, so a headless app with no text could not start there; the buffer is now made by the first shaping (Issue #460).
- Code whose shape depends on a size (a bitmap made for the glyph's size, a buffer grown for the largest so far) is tested at the small end too, and with the small case first. T6's color glyph tests started at 24 px; a first emoji under about 20 px made the bitmap again while Direct2D drew, and a measurement found it (PR #192).
- Golden images and DisplayList snapshots render with the bundled fonts only (design §12.3).
- Before a failure is taken for one that comes now and then, check whether it repeats and after which change it began. After T9, `TextSteadyStateTests` failed in every local gate; the first two were taken for Issue #92's occasional failure and the gate was run again, and only the third led to bisecting (Issue #221, PR #222).
- A test that allocates or inflates tens of megabytes runs in a collection apart from the others (`DisableParallelization`), and a large input it needs is built in pieces rather than in one array. T9's 64 MB inflate test, run beside `TextSteadyStateTests`, made that Zero-Alloc test count 1.5 to 6 KB in every local Debug run, with no collection (Issue #221, PR #222; why is row Q1's question). V1a's `TextLeafTests` allocated about 32 MB (90 trees of one text leaf, and 100 leaves laid out hundreds of times) beside the other text tests; the agent wrote them without reading this rule, and PR #304 moves them apart (Issue #302).
- The warm-up of a Zero-Alloc test over a cache that overwrites its oldest entry runs the measured loops as they are measured, until one run of them allocates nothing (with a limit). Which entry holds which key depends on the order of the runs before, and an entry grows its arrays the first time it holds a larger need. V1a's text leaf test warmed up with one run of each loop, and a later Debug gate counted 8,136 bytes in the measured run (PR #301).
- A Zero-Alloc test whose iteration takes its index from `PerformanceAssert.DoesNotAllocate` shifts it before use: the helper hands its unmeasured window negative indices. The warm-up before the helper continues the same run of indices, and the test counts the work the iterations claim to do (frames presented, entries written). V1b's text leaf frames test read an array at a negative index, and after the fix its indices went back at the measured part, so that its first 20 frames changed nothing and presented nothing (PR #313).
- A test that a subtree is skipped (`Hidden`) checks something the skipped path would do besides its pixels, such as a draw function that must not be called. The layout gives a hidden node a rectangle of 0, so a mutant that does not skip it drew no pixel and survived (PR #313).
- An object that gets a value only from a pass that runs now and then (the layout pass, a frame) is also tested on the path that makes or attaches it between two passes. Every wheel test of V2 gave the scroll container its `ScrollState` at the mount; a state the wheel attached later kept a range of 0, since a scroll position runs no layout pass, and the next wheel brought the position back to 0. The review found it (PR #348).
- A test of a failure path writes what the failure leaves (images, dumps, logs) to a folder of its own and deletes it, so the folders the CI uploads on a failed job hold only real failures. `GoldenTests` wrote the failures it meant into `GoldenFailures/`, the folder the CI uploads, beside the images of a real mismatch (the review of PR #469).
- A headless test that ends with a frame or a timer still due fails at the app's disposal (design §12.2): a click, a focus move, or a signal set asks for a frame, so such a test pumps (`PumpAndSettle`) before it ends.
- A test of what asks for a frame also changes one kind of state after another (a layout change, then a color), not each alone on a fresh tree. V1b's tests did each once, and missed that a node whose layout changed kept its paint flag outside the paint updates, so a later change of color asked for no frame; V1c's hover scene found it (Issue #324, PR #326).
- A clue added to a test that fails now and then lives in the test, not in shipped code, and is checked once with a failure caused on purpose. For Issue #126 the idle test's thread hooks record the messages the UI thread handles, and posting `WM_USER + 7` during the wait made the failure name `0x0407` (PR #217).
- Every run of `./build.ps1` writes its output to a file in the scratchpad, so that a failure seen once can be named. In T8 one run in four failed one Release test, and the output was not kept, so the test could not be named (PR #204).

## Structure

- `tests/<Project>.Tests` mirrors `src/<Project>`. `Foo.cs` → `FooTests.cs`, in the same folder.
- Method names: `{Target}_{Scenario}_{Expected}` in PascalCase, English.
- Each test owns its state. Tests run in parallel; a test that cannot (a real window, the UI thread) says why in its collection definition.

## Documentation

Every `[Fact]` and `[Theory]` has XML docs in this shape, in English:

```csharp
/// <summary>
/// One line: the behavior this test guards.
/// </summary>
/// <remarks>
/// Preconditions:
/// - ...
///
/// Steps:
/// - ...
///
/// Expected:
/// - ...
/// </remarks>
[Fact]
public void Dispose_NestedScopes_RunsChildrenThenOwnCleanupsInLifoOrder()
{
}
```

- A `[Theory]` is documented once; list what the cases cover under Preconditions or Steps.
- No custom XML tags. Link a design section when the why is not obvious.

## What a test checks

- A test stands for the whole design statement (`.cursor/rules/spec-checks.mdc`).
- The expected value of a subtle CSS result (fit-content, an automatic minimum, a percentage of an indefinite size) comes from an outside reference, not from reasoning: Chrome's result through a Taffy fixture, or the same tree laid out from the start. In L4 a test expected a fit-content width of 30 that the engine, matching the fixtures, gives as 50, and the test had to go (PR #103).
- Before the design takes Chrome as the reference for a value, make one fixture with it and check that the attribute is still in the XML: Chrome drops a declaration it does not support, and the fixture then tests `auto`. In L3 Chrome 155 dropped `fit-content(50px)` on `width`, `max-width`, and `flex-basis`, after the design had chosen Chrome as its reference; the value left the row (PR #134).
- A row checked against an outside reference also makes fixtures of its feature with each feature of an earlier row that changes the same sizes (aspect-ratio, wrapping, absolute positioning), before the PR. A Risk line that names the combinations as unchecked does not replace them. In L3 the review asked for six such fixtures, and two with an aspect ratio found that a keyword minimum did not pass through the ratio (PR #134).
- Code written to match an outside reference is ported from the version that made the expected values. In L2 the agent ported absolute positioning from Taffy 0.9.0, the version the gpui crate pins (Zed ships 0.13.0, row L9), while the fixtures come from the Taffy clone (0.15, its `oof.rs`); 30 fixtures disagreed until the port followed 0.15 (PR #125).
- Compare with an independent reference: a hand-computed layout, the Unicode test files, a golden image produced by an earlier reviewed run. Do not compare a function with a re-implementation of itself.
- A random test of incremental work puts several changes, and changes that keep a size but not what a parent reads, before one pass. In L5 the test first made one change per pass, and its "same size" change changed nothing; once it changed 1 to 3 leaves, a leaf that an ancestor laid out first gave a wrong rectangle, which no single change can reach (PR #112, #115).
- A random test of a value that is kept until it is read reads only some of the values after each step, at random, so that a value goes unread for several steps. Reading all of them clears every mark each step and never leaves one standing. V3b's test reads each scroll container's range with a chance of one half; it found the shape tried while writing (no mark above a layout root that scrolls) wrong at seed 34 (PR #377).
- The number of Taffy fixtures a row waits on is counted from every attribute of each fixture, not from the skip reason, which names only the first feature found. In L2 the 211 fixtures with an L2 reason held some that also wait on V2, P03, P02, L3, or L6; counted by every attribute, 204 use only L2's features and 28 also need another row (PR #121). The number that pass is read from the test run: the passed tests and the skipped ones with each reason. In L3 the agent wrote that 20 of the 24 `stretch` fixtures pass, while 6 of them were skipped for floats and overflow and 14 passed (PR #134), after 201 for 199 in L2.
- A pixel test that draws several shapes in one frame and compares them with a CPU reference keeps the shapes apart, their antialiased edges included. The reference adds the shapes' colors, while the renderer composites one over the other, so a pixel two shapes share differs; in R4b two arcs and two sectors that touched by a pixel, and two ellipses that crossed, failed the test, not the renderer (PR #256).
- A test of an API that takes input calls it with the input in the form it really comes in (Win32's integer client coordinates, not a pixel's center). R4e's pixel test called the hit test at each pixel's center, and missed that integer coordinates hit one pixel past a region's right and bottom edges; the review found it (PR #277).
- A function that answers from the context around a position (`Graphemes.IsBoundary`) is compared with the function that walks from the start, at every position of the conformance tests, the positions between the halves of a surrogate pair included (PR #285).
- A test of code that measures or compares floating-point widths or positions also runs at sizes that are not whole pixels (13.3 px, 15.7 px). T1b's tests shaped at 1,000 px, where a font unit is one pixel, and missed that Pass1 and Pass2 summed a line's width in different orders: at a fractional size a text broken at its own most content width had its widest line cut (PR #293).
- A tolerance (pixel difference, float comparison) is a fixed number written in the test, with the reason in a comment when it is not the default.
- A check for NaN looks at each value: a rectangle with infinite edges (no clip) sums to NaN, since -∞ + ∞ is NaN. In R4d a test summed the four edges of a state's clip and failed on a state with no clip (PR #268).
- The setup that a mutant needs reaches the branch it changes, and the test asserts that it does. In S8a an atlas "full" for 28-pixel glyphs still had room for a 0-pixel blank glyph, so the mutant that made blanks take room passed; the test now checks where the next glyph lands. In S9 a scope's cleanup ran with the current scope of the code that disposed it, so a test that disposed from outside never reached the "a scope being disposed takes no children" branch; the test now disposes the scope from its own body. In V3b the test of a reused slot changed the scroll container's padding to mark it, which laid the box out again too, so the freed box's memo was already stale and the mutant that kept it passed; changing a sibling leaf instead, and asserting that the box's memo is still known, made it fail (PR #377).
- A test that edits a state through steps lets the frames process each step before the next (a pump, a layout pass): a cache is made only from a state that went through a frame. P02b's tests typed into a text field and emptied it before any frame shaped the typed text, so an emptied leaf had no old shaping result to keep, and the tests passed while the todo field drew the added name after its text was emptied; the owner found it on the real machine (PR #492).
- A memo or cache key of several values is tested by changing each value alone, the others kept. In L7 the first test changed the container's width and its border together with the static positions, so mutants that dropped the comparison of the container size or of the border passed; a step that changed only the height, and one that traded padding for border, made all five mutants fail (PR #138).
- A surviving mutant is first a question about the setup (the item above). When the branch cannot be reached at all, show why and remove the branch instead of listing the mutant (`.cursor/rules/no-dead-code.mdc`). In T13 the mutants that dropped each inline object's own content widths survived: a text measured at a min-content width measures its children at one too, so the rooms already had those widths; the computation went, and with it two measurements of each child (PR #438). In S9 relinking a computed value always follows a refresh at the same global version, so the code that marked it dirty there went. A script that tries every small input can show it: in V3a the diff's check that left a row off the longest increasing subsequence in place when it already sat after the previous row never applied in 178,924 such rows, from every order of up to 7 rows with new rows mixed in, and went; Vue moves every such row (PR #369).
- A test of a comparison (a least-recently-used pick, a priority, a tie-break) sets up candidates whose values differ. In S8b every plot of the eviction test had the same age, so the plot picked first was also the oldest, and the mutant that picked the newest passed; the test now gives the plots three different ages.
- The order between a node's handlers and a default action at the same node (a scroll container's handler before its scrolling) is tested with the handler on that node itself. K1b's test put the stopping handler on the container's child, whose bubble handler runs first in either order, and the mutant that scrolled before the handlers survived (PR #360).
- A handler that watches what a scope's disposal does is added in a scope that outlives it: a handler is not called while its own scope is being disposed (`Scope.TryEnter` in `FocusManager.Invoke`). V3a's first test of a `For` disposed with a focused row disposed the root scope, which also held the handler, and no focus-out arrived; the test now disposes only the scope that holds the `For` (PR #369).
- A promise about order across several events (the states change before both the focus-out and the focus-in) is checked inside the handler of each event it names. K1a's test read the states in the focus-in alone, and a mutant that set the new node's states after the focus-out survived (PR #356).
- An OS behavior that a test cannot make with `SendMessage` (what `DefWindowProc` does from the real keyboard's state, such as closing on Alt+F4) is named in the test's remarks and in the PR as not tested, and the test checks what it can make: the result the window returns. K1a's first test sent Alt+F4 to a hidden window and waited for it to close; it did not close, even after the posted messages were dispatched (PR #356).
- A pixel test compares the frame with an image composed on the CPU from the same inputs in the display list's order, not each element with the background around it. Neighbouring glyph boxes overlap, so in S8b a check of the ring around each glyph failed on the next glyph's ink.
- Mutation check for a guard: change the guarded term and see the test fail, then restore it by copying the file aside and back (not `git checkout`). List the mutations in the PR.
- A mutant must build. With warnings as errors, a mutant such as `if (false)` fails to compile (CS0162), `dotnet test --no-build` then runs the previous binaries, and the mutant looks survived. Check the build's exit code before reading the test result, and write the mutant so that it compiles (`if (IsActive && count < 0)`) (S4). A style analyzer fails the build too: in S5 a mutant with a trailing space (SA1028) did not build. A mutant run that names no failing test counts as a failed build until its build output says otherwise. Read this item before writing the mutants: in S8b the first mutants were `if (true)` again and did not build, and in I1a three of 23 were `if (false)` again; a false condition the compiler cannot fold (`pointer.PressedCount < 0`, `info.pointerFlags == 0xFFFFFFFF`) built (PR #389).
- A script that runs mutants decides from the exit code of `dotnet test`, not from its text: Microsoft.Testing.Platform exits with 2 when a test failed, and its messages follow the OS language. `python -I` ignores `PYTHONIOENCODING`; a script that prints test output calls `sys.stdout.reconfigure(encoding="utf-8", errors="replace")` (S8b). In V2 a mutant script searched the output for `[FAIL]`; on this machine the runner writes `失敗 <test>`, so every mutant read as 0 failures until the output was read by hand (PR #348).
- A mutant that a lower layer hides from the test's output is checked on that layer's class directly. I1c's mutant that let the OS's inertia queue pan updates survived the window's tests, since the window drops the updates of a pan that ended; a test of `TouchpadGestures` alone counts the queue (PR #410).
- A test that hangs on the CI is found by the test names that `--output Detailed` prints as each test ends (`build.ps1` passes it, but for the text tests): the one not printed hung. xUnit's `longRunningTestSeconds` and `--long-running` print nothing through Microsoft.Testing.Platform's output, which a test that sleeps showed (PR #410).
- A mutant that would dereference null in unsafe code is not run: it ends the test process and the other tests do not run. List it in the PR with that reason. A mutant that only leaks a COM reference cannot be caught without the D3D11 debug layer; say so too (S7).
- Every test of a contract the design states gets that mutation check before the PR, not only the guards a review names. A test that passes against the mutant passes for another reason, and the reason is usually the test's own setup or cleanup.
- Where several things ask for frames, a test that some work asks for no more of them first runs the frames that the other work asked for, names them in a comment, and then asserts that no frame is pending. I1b's test of a fling that does not start checked `CountingScheduler.Pending` right after the release, and the frame that the release's own move asked for kept it set; the test now runs one frame and then checks (`TouchScrollTests.Fling_OnlyPastFluttersConditions_AndStopsAtTheEnd`, PR #397).
- Read the state you assert before a shared helper's cleanup runs. A helper that resets state on the way out (`RunLoop` cancels the pending frame in its `finally`) makes an assertion after it check the helper, not the code. Record the state inside the run, where the code under test left it.

```csharp
// BAD — RunLoop's finally calls CancelFrame, which stops the clock: this passes even when RunFrame never stops it (S3)
RunLoop(watchdog, () => runs >= 1, () => frames.RequestFrame(callback));
Assert.False(frames.IsClockRunning);

// GOOD — read the clock in the iteration after the frame, before the cleanup
bool? runningAfterFrame = null;
RunLoop(watchdog, () => { if (runs < 1) return false; runningAfterFrame = frames.IsClockRunning; return true; }, start);
Assert.False(runningAfterFrame);
```
